<!-- SECTION 8: DOCUMENTS REQUIRED -->
<section class="content-section" id="documents-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
                <h2 class="section-title" id="documents-section">Documents Required for GSTAT Appeal Filing at Kochi</h2><ul><li>Form APL-05 (filed electronically on <a href="https://efiling.gstat.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">efiling.gstat.gov.in</a>)</li><li>Certified copy of impugned order from Appellate or Revisional Authority</li><li>Pre-deposit payment proof from Electronic Cash Ledger</li><li>GSTIN registration certificate</li><li>Returns (GSTR-1, GSTR-3B, GSTR-9) for disputed period</li><li>Show Cause Notice (SCN) and reply</li><li>Assessment or adjudication order</li><li>Vakalatnama in GSTAT FORM-04 stamped per Kerala High Court rules</li><li>Affidavit supporting the appeal</li><li>All documents in English or Hindi</li></ul><div class="highlight-box" style="background:var(--orange-light);border-left:4px solid var(--orange);padding:16px 20px;border-radius:var(--radius-md);margin-top:20px;"><p style="font-weight:600;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Kerala-specific Requirements</p><p>Malayalam-language orders from Kerala State GST require certified English or Hindi translations. Spice trade appellants should include quality certificates, FSSAI documentation, and export invoices. IT/ITES businesses must provide SAC evidence and service agreements. Lakshadweep businesses must identify CGST vs UTGST - respondent differs from KGST. See <a href="/gst-returns">GST return filing</a> for documentation.</p></div>
            </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 9: CHALLENGES -->
<section class="content-section" id="challenges-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
                <h2 class="section-title" id="challenges-section">Challenges in GSTAT Appeal Filing at Kochi Bench</h2><h3>Circuit Bench Routing</h3><p>Businesses in Thiruvananthapuram, Kollam, Alappuzha, Pathanamthitta, and Kottayam must file at the Thiruvananthapuram circuit, not Kochi. Businesses in Ernakulam, Thrissur, or Palakkad must file at Kochi main bench. Filing at the wrong location causes procedural delays. See also <a href="/gst-audit">GST audit support</a>.</p><h3>Three CGST Commissionerates</h3><p>Kerala has 3 CGST Commissionerates (Kochi, Thiruvananthapuram, Kozhikode) covering different regions. Correctly identifying which commissionerate passed the original order is essential for naming the respondent.</p><h3>Lakshadweep UTGST Distinction</h3><p>Lakshadweep applies UTGST (not KGST). Geographic isolation means physical hearing is impractical. Hybrid mode is essential. The respondent authority differs from Kerala's KGST structure.</p><h3>Malayalam Translation Requirement</h3><p>Kerala State GST orders may be in Malayalam. The GSTAT portal accepts English/Hindi only. Translation timelines must be factored within the 3-month limitation period.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:var(--blue-lighter);border-left:4px solid var(--blue);padding:16px 20px;border-radius:var(--radius-md);margin-top:20px;"><p style="font-weight:600;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Illustrative Scenario</p><p>An IT company in Infopark Kochi receives an Rs 42 lakh ITC reversal demand from the Kochi CGST Commissionerate. The 20% pre-deposit amounts to Rs 8.4 lakh. Filing is at the Kochi main bench. A spice exporter in Kottayam with a classification dispute must file at the Thiruvananthapuram circuit (not Kochi), and Malayalam-language orders require translation. Patron's team handles both scenarios with correct routing and sector-specific expertise.</p></div>
            </div>
    </div>
</section>

