                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Which districts fall under the Kochi GSTAT bench jurisdiction?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Kochi (Ernakulam) main bench covers 9 Kerala districts and Lakshadweep UT. Thiruvananthapuram circuit covers 5 southern Kerala districts: Thiruvananthapuram, Kollam, Alappuzha, Pathanamthitta, and Kottayam. Together, all 14 Kerala districts and Lakshadweep are covered.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Is the Kochi GSTAT bench operational?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Members allotted via GSTAT Order No. 03/2025 dated 26 December 2025, directed to join 21 January 2026. Thiruvananthapuram circuit is operational per GSTAT President directions based on appeal volume. Hearings in hybrid mode.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the pre-deposit for filing at Kochi GSTAT bench?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>20% of disputed tax under S.112(8) CGST/KGST Act (cumulative). For Lakshadweep, UTGST applies. Cap Rs 20 crore each. For penalty-only demands, 10%. Non-payment results in rejection at admission.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the deadline for filing at Kochi GSTAT bench?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>3 months from date of order under S.112(1). Condonation under S.112(4) is discretionary. Backlog appeals before 1 April 2026: deadline 30 June 2026. Factor Malayalam translation time within the limitation window.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">How does the Thiruvananthapuram circuit bench work?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>The Thiruvananthapuram circuit is a sitting arrangement of the Kochi bench. Cases from Thiruvananthapuram, Kollam, Alappuzha, Pathanamthitta, and Kottayam are heard there. Operational schedule depends on appeal volume. These 5 districts must file at the circuit, not Kochi.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Does the Kochi bench cover Lakshadweep?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Lakshadweep falls under Kochi main bench. Lakshadweep businesses are subject to UTGST not KGST. Respondent authority is CGST or UTGST administrator. Hybrid hearing mode essential for island appellants given geographic isolation.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can I appeal a Kochi GSTAT order to the Kerala High Court?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Under S.113, appeal lies to Kerala HC at Ernakulam on questions of law. Both GSTAT bench and Kerala HC are in Ernakulam - making Kochi one of India's most appellate-friendly GSTAT jurisdictions. No inter-city travel for S.113 appeals.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-8">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What CGST commissionerates operate in the Kochi bench jurisdiction?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Three CGST Commissionerates under Chief Commissioner, CGST, Kochi Zone. Kochi Commissionerate covers Ernakulam, Thrissur, Idukki, Palakkad. Thiruvananthapuram covers 5 southern districts. Kozhikode covers Malabar coast districts. Correct commissionerate must be named as respondent.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight-box" style="background:var(--gray-50);padding:24px;border-radius:var(--radius-md);margin-top:24px;"><h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:22px;margin-bottom:16px;">Quick Answers - GSTAT Appeal Filing in Kochi</h2><div class="qa-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:16px;"><div><p style="font-weight:700;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Can I file offline?</p><p>No. Rule 18 mandates online filing via efiling.gstat.gov.in.</p></div><div><p style="font-weight:700;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Is Kottayam under Kochi or TVM circuit?</p><p>Kottayam is under the Thiruvananthapuram circuit, not Kochi main bench.</p></div><div><p style="font-weight:700;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Are Malayalam orders accepted?</p><p>GSTAT accepts English and Hindi. Malayalam orders require certified translation.</p></div><div><p style="font-weight:700;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Is Lakshadweep treated as Kerala?</p><p>Lakshadweep falls under Kochi bench but applies UTGST (not KGST). Respondent differs.</p></div><div><p style="font-weight:700;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Where is Kerala HC?</p><p>At Ernakulam (Kochi) - co-located with GSTAT bench for convenient S.113 appeals.</p></div></div></div>
        <div class="highlight-box" style="background:var(--blue-lighter);border-radius:var(--radius-md);padding:24px;margin-top:32px;">
    <h3 style="color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:16px;">Quick Answers</h3>
    <div class="qa-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:16px;">
        <div><strong>When does NAAR start?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">GSTAT Principal Bench acts as NAAR from April 2026 per 56th GST Council recommendation.</span></div>
        <div><strong>What is the NAAR appeal deadline?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">30 days from AAAR order under Section 101B. Condonation up to 30 additional days.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Who can file a NAAR appeal?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">Any person affected by conflicting AAAR rulings in two or more states.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Is there a pre-deposit?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">No  -  only Rs 10,000 filing fee under CGST.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Where is NAAR located?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">GSTAT Principal Bench at New Delhi.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Can a single adverse ruling be appealed to NAAR?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">No  -  conflicting rulings from 2+ states are required. Single adverse ruling remedy is HC writ.</span></div>
